[2m• [0mEvery evening, Mara climbed the lighthouse stairs with a lantern wick, a kettle, and a letter she never sent. The
  letter was addressed to her brother, lost at sea twelve winters ago. She wrote about ordinary things: the gull that
  stole her lunch, the cracked blue cup, the storms that rattled the windows. Then she folded it and tucked it beneath
  the lamp.

  One November night, fog swallowed the coast. The warning bell jammed, and the great beam faltered. Far below, a
  fishing boat drifted toward the black teeth of the reef. Mara pulled the bell rope until her palms blistered. Nothing.
  She seized the brass mirror used for signaling and angled it toward the moon.

  A flash answered from the boat.

  Mara turned the mirror again, steadying it against the wind. Three flashes came back, then a lantern appeared, moving
  slowly toward the safe channel. At dawn, the fishermen climbed the lighthouse steps, soaked and shivering. Their
  youngest carried a small envelope.

  “Found this caught in our net,” he said.

  Inside was Mara’s old letter, softened by seawater. On its blank back, someone had written: “I read every word. Keep
  the light on.”

  Mara looked toward the empty horizon, then climbed to the lamp. For the first time, she left the letter unfolded
  beneath its bright, turning heart.

